      "text": "Let $\\alpha = r(\\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta)$ and $\\beta = r'(\\cos \\theta' + i \\sin \\theta')$ as in the original solution.  Then\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta^2} &= \\frac{r \\left( \\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta \\right)}{r'^2 \\left( \\cos^2 \\theta' - \\frac{1}{2} i \\cos \\theta' \\sin \\theta' - \\frac{1}{2} \\sin^2 \\theta' \\right)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{r \\left( \\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta \\right)}{r' \\left( 1 - \\cos \\theta' \\right)}.\n\\end{align*}Since $\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta^2}$ is real, $i$ must divide the numerator, so $\\cos \\theta' = 1,$ and\n\\[\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta^2} = \\frac{r \\left( \\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta \\right)}{r' (1 - \\cos \\theta')}. \\quad \\text{(1)}\\]Since $|\\alpha - \\beta| = 2 \\sqrt{3},$ $|\\cos \\theta - \\cos \\theta'| = \\sqrt{3},$ and so\n\\[\\sqrt{2 \\left( \\cos^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta' \\right) + 2 \\left( \\cos \\theta' \\right) (-1) \\left( \\cos \\theta \\right)} = \\sqrt{6}. \\quad \\text{(2)}\\]Using Pythagoras on (2) gives\n\\[\\cos \\theta + \\cos \\theta' = 1,\\]and so $\\frac{1}{r} + \\frac{1}{r'} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$  Substituting $\\cos \\theta = 1 - \\cos \\theta'$ into (1) gives\n\\[\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta^2} = \\frac{r \\left( \\frac{\\cos \\theta}{r'} \\right)}{\\left( 1 - \\cos \\theta' \\right)}. \\quad \\text{(3)}\\]We want to match the denominators in (1) and (3).  Compare the top of (3) to the numerator of (1):\n\\[\\frac{r \\left( \\sqrt{2} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)}{r'} \\text{ and }\\frac{r' \\left( \\sqrt{2} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)}{r'}.\\]Then $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = 0,$ which is absurd.  Then the bottom of (3) must match the bottom of (1):\n\\[\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sqrt{^{3}-^{2}}} \\text{ and } 1 + \\cos \\theta'.\\]Finally, this is the equality $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{1}{r'},$ and\n\\[|\\alpha| = r = \\frac{|\\cos \\theta|}{1 + \\cos \\theta'} = \\boxed{\\frac{5}{6}}.\\]. The answer is \\frac{5}{6}",
