      "text": "Given the conjugate complex numbers $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ such that $\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta^2}$ is a real number, and $|\\alpha - \\beta| = \\sqrt{12}$.\n\n1. Let's represent $\\alpha$ as $a + bi$ and $\\beta$ as $c + di$, where $a, b, c, d \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Since $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are conjugate complex numbers, the imaginary part of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ must be equal.\n\n2. Suppose $\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta^2} = k \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then, $\\alpha = k \\beta^2$ or $\\alpha = -k \\beta^2$.\n\n3. Since $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are conjugate, their difference is twice the real part of $\\alpha$:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha - \\beta = (k \\beta^2 - \\beta) \\Rightarrow (\\alpha - \\beta) \\cdot \\beta = (\\alpha - \\beta) \\cdot c\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute $\\alpha = k \\beta^2$ into the above equation:\n   \\begin{align*}\n   (\\alpha - \\beta) \\cdot \\beta &= (\\alpha - \\beta) \\cdot c \\\\\n   (k \\beta^2 - \\beta) \\cdot \\beta &= (k \\beta^2 - \\beta) \\cdot c \\\\\n   k \\beta^3 - \\beta^2 &= k \\beta^3 - c \\beta^2 \\\\\n   -\\beta^2 &= -c \\beta^2 \\\\\n   c &= 1\n   \\end{align*}\n\n5. Given $|\\alpha - \\beta| = \\sqrt{12}$, substitute $\\alpha = k \\beta^2$ and $|\\beta| = 1$ into the equation:\n   \\[\n   |k \\beta^2 - \\beta| = \\sqrt{12}\n   \\]\n   Let $|\\beta| = r$. Since $\\beta$ is a complex number, $|\\beta| = 1$, so we have:\n   \\[\n   |k \\beta^2 - \\beta| = |k| = \\sqrt{12}\n   \\]\n\n6. Since $|\\beta| = 1$, we can rewrite:\n   \\[\n   |\\beta^2|\\cdot |k| = |\\beta^2 - \\beta| = \\sqrt{12}\n   \\]\n\n7. Hence, $|1||k| = |\\beta^2 - \\beta| = \\sqrt{12}$, thus $|k| = \\sqrt{12}$.\n\n8. Now, since $|k| = \\sqrt{12}$, we find $|\\alpha| = |k| \\cdot |\\beta| = \\sqrt{12} \\cdot 1 = \\boxed{2\\sqrt{3}}$.",
